<v Starlee Kine>Everything's exactly the same. Except this time, instead of a young Sean Penn, it's a young Crispin Glover, Crispin Glover. That's right, the guy that played Michael   J. Fox's dad in Back to the Future .
<v Starlee Kine>一切都完全一样。只是这一次，不是年轻的肖恩·潘，而是年轻的克里斯平·格洛弗，克里斯平·格洛弗。没错，就是在《回到未来》里演迈克尔·J·福克斯父亲的那个家伙。

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:39.430
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>Well, let's see here. Here's a  little bit of the old John Wayne for you. Well, let me tell ya, something out there in TV land. I just made the tube, and it just tickles the heck right out of me.
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>嗯，让我们看看。这是给你们的一点老约翰·韦恩的表演。好吧，让我告诉你们，电视机前的各位。我刚上了电视，这真是让我高兴得不得了。

00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:42.850
<v Starlee Kine>The rest is the same too. There's Crispin in the mortuary.
<v Starlee Kine>其余的也一样。克里斯平也在停尸房。

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:46.600
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>Guess I'd better get into my threads.
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>我想我最好去换上行头。

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:49.850
<v Starlee Kine>There's Crispin at the town talent show.
<v Starlee Kine>克里斯平也在镇上的才艺秀上。

00:19:49.850 --> 00:19:59.810
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>[SINGING]
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>（唱歌）

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:48.240
<v Starlee Kine>But while the first two versions of the story were basically made with a home video camera and looked it, this one looks like a real movie. It's shot on film. There's lighting and extras and actual sets. It also cost a lot more. Trent spent $50,000 on it. And, as is often the case in Hollywood, the big budget remake is not as good as the original. Trent adds a whole new backstory to make the Beaver Kid seem like an heroic outsider. People are constantly mocking him, sticking tacks on his chair, telling him he'll never make it onto TV. And the director is an oily, villainous, mustachioed type name Terrance. And just like in the Sean Penn version, Crispin pulls out a gun after the talent show and considers killing himself. He calls the director and begs him not to put the footage on TV. [PHONE RINGING]
<v Starlee Kine>但是，当故事的前两个版本基本上是用家庭摄像机拍摄并带有家庭录像感时，这一个看起来就像真正的电影。它是胶片拍摄的。有灯光、临时演员和真正的场景。它也花了很多钱。特伦特花了5万美元。而且，就像好莱坞经常发生的那样，大制作的重拍版并没有原版好。特伦特增加了一个全新的背景故事，让比弗男孩看起来像个英雄般的局外人。人们不断嘲笑他，在他的椅子上放图钉，告诉他永远别想上电视。导演是一个油头粉面、留着胡子、名叫特伦斯的恶棍角色。就像肖恩·潘的版本一样，克里斯平在才艺秀后掏出了枪，考虑自杀。他给导演打电话，乞求他不要把录像放上电视。（电话铃响）

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:49.740
<v Ken Butler In The Beaver Trilogy>Hello?
<v Ken Butler In The Beaver Trilogy>喂？

00:20:49.740 --> 00:21:11.220
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>Terrance, how ya pal? I hope you don't think I'm crazy for this, but I'm a little worried about that Olivia number. And I'd be willing to pay for your gas, and film and stuff if you just wouldn't put it on TV.
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>特伦斯，伙计，你怎么样？希望你别觉得我疯了，但我有点担心那个奥利维亚的部分。如果你不把它放上电视，我愿意支付你的油费、胶片费等等。

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:23.860
<v Ken Butler In The Beaver Trilogy>I put a lot of time and effort into this project. Look, people are going to love it. You look great. And besides, I got a deadline to meet. Would I lie to you?
<v Ken Butler In The Beaver Trilogy>我在这个项目上花了很多时间和精力。听着，人们会喜欢的。你看起来很棒。再说，我还要赶截止日期。我会骗你吗？

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:39.840
<v Trent Harris>I emerge as a character in this series. And I guess my character is just kind of a jerk, for lack of a better word. He is very insensitive and very, sort of, just after a story and nothing else, and very exploitative.
<v Trent Harris>我成了这个系列里的一个角色。我想我的角色简直就是个混蛋，找不到更好的词来形容。他非常麻木不仁，而且非常、某种程度上、只是为了一个故事而不择手段，非常剥削人。

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:59.810
<v Starlee Kine>When I asked Trent why he felt the need to add the phone call, and the gun, and exploitative director character, at first he says, it was just for dramatic effect. He tells me every movie needs a villain. He tells me reality doesn't have anything to do with anything. He tells me to shut up. And then, finally, an hour into our interview, he tells me this.
<v Starlee Kine>当我问特伦特为什么觉得有必要加上那个电话、那把枪和那个剥削人的导演角色时，起初他说，那只是为了戏剧效果。他告诉我每部电影都需要一个反派。他告诉我现实和这一切毫无关系。他让我闭嘴。然后，终于，在采访进行了一个小时后，他告诉了我这个。

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:31.610
<v Trent Harris>What happened? I did get a phone call. I did get a phone call after I'd been in Beaver. And the phone call said, listen I've been thinking about that, maybe you shouldn't put it on TV. And I remember saying, oh, don't worry about it. It's going to be fine. And maybe I wasn't as sensitive as I should have been or could have been. Not too long after that, I found out that he'd been shot.
<v Trent Harris>发生了什么？我确实接到了一个电话。我确实在比弗之后接到了一个电话。那个电话说，听着，我一直在想那件事，也许你不应该把它放上电视。我记得我说，哦，别担心。会没事的。也许我当时不够敏感，没有达到我应该或者可以达到的程度。没过多久，我就发现他中枪了。

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:38.800
<v Starlee Kine>Trent called the hospital, and was told the Beaver Kid had an accident with a gun. The kid was OK though, able to talk on the phone.
<v Starlee Kine>特伦特打电话给医院，得知比弗男孩发生了枪支意外。不过孩子没事，还能在电话里说话。

00:22:38.800 --> 00:23:02.220
<v Trent Harris>And then he just kept apologizing to me, which was kind of very confusing. Very sorry that he'd put me through what he was putting me through. Which seemed kind of odd at the time, because I felt like I should be apologizing to him. I guess I felt like maybe I'd pushed him a little bit too much.
<v Trent Harris>然后他就不停地向我道歉，这让我感到非常困惑。很抱歉他经历了我让他经历的一切。这在当时看起来很奇怪，因为我觉得应该是我向他道歉才对。我想我可能觉得我有点太逼他了。

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:20.990
<v Starlee Kine>The Beaver Kid declined my request for an interview. After all that, Trent quit his TV job, never airing his footage of the talent show, and drove to Hollywood where he immediately started working on the Sean Penn version of the story. Still thinking about how he'd originally ignored the kid's request not to broadcast the footage and how the kid ended up in the hospital.
<v Starlee Kine>比弗男孩拒绝了我的采访请求。在那之后，特伦特辞掉了电视台的工作，从没播出过那段才艺秀的录像，并开车去了好莱坞，在那里他立即开始制作故事的肖恩·潘版本。他依然在想，当初他是如何无视那个孩子不要播出录像的请求，以及那个孩子最终是如何住进医院的。

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:24.400
<v Starlee Kine>Do you connect the two things together?
<v Starlee Kine>你把这两件事联系在一起了吗？

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:39.600
<v Trent Harris>I mean, in my mind, I connect them together. I don't know whether it's true or not, but in my mind I connect it together. Again, I'm very, very reluctant to talk about what is going on in his mind or to speculate that way. I don't think it's fair.
<v Trent Harris>我是说，在我心里，我把它们联系在一起。我不知道这是不是真的，但在我心里我把它们联系在一起。再说一次，我非常、非常不愿谈论他心里在想什么，或者那样揣测。我觉得这不公平。

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:44.380
<v Starlee Kine>I mean, but it seems more about you though. It seems more about--
<v Starlee Kine>我是说，但这看起来更像是关于你自己的故事。看起来更像是关于——

00:23:44.380 --> 00:24:21.690
<v Trent Harris>If you're asking me, and I think you're trying to, if I feel guilty about anything that has happened? The answer to that is yes. Of course, I do feel guilty about things. I feel guilty about putting myself and my needs above other people's needs, above his needs. I wasn't appreciative of that, and  sensitive of that. And for that, I've always felt guilty. And there you have it. That's why I keep making this damn thing over and over again. Now can we quit?
<v Trent Harris>如果你是在问我，而且我想你是想问，我对所发生的任何事情是否感到内疚？答案是肯定的。当然，我对某些事情感到内疚。我对自己把我和我的需求置于他人的需求、置于他的需求之上的行为感到内疚。我对此不够感激，也不够敏感。为此，我一直感到内疚。好了，这就是原因。这就是我为什么一次又一次地制作这该死的东西。现在我们可以结束了吗？

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:41.030
<v Starlee Kine>You know, you're the one who made these movies. You set yourself up for this whole chain of events here. So then, did you feel better after?
<v Starlee Kine>你知道，是你制作了这些电影。是你为这一整串事件搭建了舞台。那么，在那之后你感觉好点了吗？

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:47.340
<v Trent Harris>Right now, I feel terrible. It's getting worse.
<v Trent Harris>现在，我感觉糟透了。情况越来越糟。

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:54.000
<v Starlee Kine>Good, I think we're having a breakthrough here.
<v Starlee Kine>好，我想我们有了突破。

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:57.430
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>How about a cup to go?
<v Crispin Glover In The Beaver Trilogy>来杯外带的怎么样？

00:24:57.430 --> 00:25:24.270
<v Starlee Kine>The third version of the story, like the Sean Penn version, has a happy ending. Crispin Glover wears his Olivia Newton-John outfit out in public. He doesn't care what people think anymore, and then he literally drives off into the sunset. [MOTORCYCLE REVVING AND  MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Starlee Kine>故事的第三个版本，像肖恩·潘的版本一样，有一个圆满的结局。克里斯平·格洛弗公开穿着他的奥利维亚·纽顿-约翰服装。他不再在乎别人怎么想，然后他真的开车驶向了落日。（摩托车轰鸣声和音乐声）

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:36.500
<v Starlee Kine>The screen fades to black. When I saw this in the theater, everyone in the audience hesitated for a minute before standing up. We didn't know who might walk out onto that parking lot next.
<v Starlee Kine>屏幕淡入黑色。当我在电影院看到这一幕时，观众席上的每个人都迟疑了一分钟才站起来。我们不知道下一个谁会走出来走向那个停车场。

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:55.920
<v Starlee Kine>What's so crazy about Trent spending years making the exact same movie over and over, is not what he added to the film each time, but what he kept the same. Trent could've shot anywhere in the world, anything he wanted, but instead he kept coming back to the same drab parking lot. Details that would seem totally random to anyone watching, he repeats.
<v Starlee Kine>特伦特多年来一遍又一遍地制作完全一样的电影，最疯狂的地方不在于他每次在电影里增加了什么，而在于他保留了什么。特伦特本可以在世界上任何地方、拍任何他想拍的东西，但他却不断地回到同一个沉闷的停车场。对于任何观看的人来说，那些细节显得完全随机，但他却不断重复。

00:25:55.920 --> 00:26:18.730
<v Starlee Kine>Like any obsession, it's always the most mundane things you can't shake. So what you end up with is a  series of movies that only make sense to the person who made them. The same story, over and over, with inexplicably happy endings thrown on. If I hadn't talked to Trent, I still would've loved these movies, but I would have had no idea what they were about. But that's OK. Because they weren't meant for us anyway.
<v Starlee Kine>像任何痴迷一样，你总是无法摆脱那些最平凡的事情。所以你最终得到的是一系列只有制作者本人才理解的电影。同样的故事，一遍又一遍，强加上了莫名其妙的圆满结局。如果我没有和特伦特谈过，我仍然会喜欢这些电影，但我会完全不知道它们是关于什么的。但这没关系。因为它们本来就不是给我们看的。

00:26:18.730 --> 00:26:48.090
<v Starlee Kine>After Trent finished the last version of the film in 1985, he did the same thing that he'd done with the first two, absolutely nothing. 13 years later, he came across all three of them in his closet and hooked them together onto one reel for the first time. He screened it at a local theater in Utah. One thing led to another, and he was invited to screen it at the Sundance Film Festival. For the first time in 22 years, Trent had to find the Beaver Kid. He had a friend track him down to invite him to Sundance.
<v Starlee Kine>特伦特在1985年完成电影的最后一个版本后，做了他和前两个版本所做的一样的事情，什么都没做。13年后，他在壁橱里翻出了这三部电影，并第一次把它们接成了一盘磁带。他在犹他州的一家当地剧院进行了放映。事情就这样发生了，他被邀请在圣丹斯电影节上放映它。22年来，特伦特第一次不得不去找比弗男孩。他找了个朋友跟踪他，邀请他去圣丹斯。

00:26:48.090 --> 00:27:13.840
<v Trent Harris>So I mean, you've got to imagine this. You've got to take this into perspective. Is that he doesn't know. This happens in 1979 and in 2001 he gets a phone call that says this is going to play at the Sundance Film Festival. Well, just before the show starts, the tickets haven't been picked up. What happens is that we show the movie. It's a huge screening. There are 1,400 people there.
<v Trent Harris>所以我是说，你必须想象一下这个场景。你必须客观看待它。他不知道。这一切发生在1979年，而在2001年他接到一个电话说这将在圣丹斯电影节播出。嗯，就在演出开始前，票还没被领走。结果是我们放映了电影。这是一场盛大的放映。有1400人在场。

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:36.200
<v Trent Harris>And after the screening, there's a bunch of people run up to the stage and ask more questions, stuff like that. All of a sudden, out of this sea of faces, one emerges. And he says, you probably don't remember me. And I immediately recognized who he was. And I grabbed him and dragged him out the side door into a snow drift.
<v Trent Harris>放映结束后，有一群人跑到舞台上问更多的问题，诸如此类的。突然，在这片人海中，一个人出现了。他说，你大概不记得我了。我立刻认出了他是谁。我抓住他，把他拖出侧门，拖进了雪堆里。

00:27:36.200 --> 00:28:03.790
<v Trent Harris>There was a lot of, huh, well, I mean, what have you-- well, I mean, wow-- That was really something. I can't believe. And then I'd say something like, yeah, I mean, wow, I tried to find you, but I didn't know where you were. And look at all this snow, and here comes a bunch of people. And then he'd say, oh my gosh, and I still have that car. And oh, you must think I'm some sort or a nut. And what in the heck is going on? Where all these people coming from? Do they think I'm some sort of a nut? Ans I said, No, no, everybody loves you, it's great. I mean, it was that kind of a conversation.
<v Trent Harris>有很多，呃，好吧，我是说，你有什么——好吧，我是说，哇——那真是了不起。我简直不敢相信。然后我会说点什么，比如，是啊，我是说，哇，我试着找过你，但我不知道你在哪儿。看看这些雪，然后来了一群人。然后他会说，天哪，而且我还有那辆车。哦，你一定觉得我是个疯子。这到底是怎么回事？所有这些人从哪儿来的？他们觉得我是个疯子吗？我说，不，不，每个人都爱你，这很棒。我是说，那是那种谈话。

00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:26.560
<v Trent Harris>And then we're mobbed. All of the sudden, there's 50 people standing around taking pictures and asking for his autograph. He doen't know. He just starts signing autographs, and then kind of slipped right into it as if he'd been doing it for years.
<v Trent Harris>然后我们被团团围住。突然，有50个人站在周围拍照，要他的签名。他不知道怎么回事。他只是开始签名，然后就像他已经做了多年一样顺其自然地融入了其中。

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:57.030
<v Trent Harris>After the screening, I took him to the big Sundance party at the end of the festival. There is a huge, huge party under a big tent. The room's filled with movie stars and all kinds of glamorous people drinking champagne and having a heck of time. And nobody cared about the movie stars. They were all around him. They're asking him questions and getting his autograph. And beautiful women hanging on his arm and running off to get him a glass of beer. It was incredible. It was great. Literally, he was the biggest star in the room.
<v Trent Harris>放映结束后，我带他去了电影节结束时的盛大圣丹斯派对。在大帐篷下有一个巨大的、巨大的派对。房间里挤满了电影明星和各种各样迷人的、喝着香槟、玩得极其开心的家伙。没人关心那些电影明星。他们都围着他。他们问他问题，要他的签名。漂亮的女人挽着他的胳膊，跑去给他拿啤酒。这真是太不可思议了。这太棒了。他简直就是房间里最大的明星。

00:28:57.030 --> 00:29:00.260
<v Starlee Kine>So was it a relief?
<v Starlee Kine>那么这是一种解脱吗？

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:03.510
<v Trent Harris>It was a tremendous relief for me.
<v Trent Harris>对我来说这是一种巨大的解脱。

00:29:03.510 --> 00:29:22.870
<v Starlee Kine>Trent kept remaking the same film because he wanted it to come out right. He wanted to give the kid a happy ending. And somehow, the plan worked. The Beaver Kid wanted to be a star. 22 years passed, and in the end, he was. And what happened in between doesn't have to make sense.
<v Starlee Kine>特伦特不停地重拍同一部电影，因为他想让它变得正确。他想给那个孩子一个圆满的结局。不知何故，计划成功了。比弗男孩想成为明星。22年过去了，最终，他确实成为了明星。而中间发生的事情不需要有任何逻辑。

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:32.730
<v Ira Glass>Starlee Kine. Trent Harris's Beaver Trilogy is not in distribution. It's not available in stores. It will not be coming to a theater near you. And maybe it's not over yet.
<v Ira Glass>斯塔莉·凯恩。特伦特·哈里斯的《海狸三部曲》并未发行。商店里买不到。它也不会在你附近的电影院上映。也许这一切还没结束。

00:29:32.730 --> 00:30:11.160
<v Trent Harris>He came up with an idea. He said, hey we ought to do part four. He had an idea, and I said, well, what's your idea? And he says, "Well, OK, imagine this. We're out on the sagebrush, nothing around except a long, long, dirt road. And you hear the music begin to creep up. And then over the horizon comes my car, in a cloud of dust, and I come. And I whip right up in front of the camera, and I screech to a halt. And as the dust clears, I lean out the window in my wig and I say, 'I ain't done yet.'" So we might do it this summer.
<v Trent Harris>他想出了一个主意。他说，嘿，我们应该拍第四部。他有个想法，我说，好吧，你的想法是什么？他说：“好吧，想象一下。我们在灌木丛中，周围什么都没有，只有一条长长的土路。你听到音乐开始蔓延。然后在地平线上，我的车在一团尘土中出现，我来了。我猛地停在摄像机前，发出刺耳的刹车声。随着尘土散去，我戴着假发从窗户探出头说：‘我还没完呢。’”所以我们今年夏天可能会拍。

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:11.970
<v Starlee Kine>You might?
<v Starlee Kine>你们可能？

00:30:11.970 --> 00:30:23.780
<v Trent Harris>Of course I will. What? You have to film that. That sounds great.
<v Trent Harris>当然会。什么？你必须拍下来。听起来很棒。

00:30:23.780 --> 00:31:42.020
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, sticking around after the break, is that a heroic act? A [? can do ?], starring the Montgomery Bus Boycott. We are pro and con-- well, mostly con actually. When we return in a minute. From Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，休息后，那是一种英雄行为吗？一场运动，主演是蒙哥马利公交车抵制运动。我们有赞成和反对——好吧，其实大多是反对。我们稍后回来。来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际公司，我们的节目继续。

00:31:42.020 --> 00:32:10.040
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. If you're just tuning in today, we are talking about reruns, personal reruns. We've arrive at Act 2 of our program. Act Two, Marriage As Rerun. And let us begin this act with this scene of people stuck in personal reruns. A scene, I would bet, that you have probably been in yourself at one point or another.
<v Ira Glass>这是《这就是美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，为你带来各种关于该主题的故事。如果你刚刚收听，我们今天谈论的是重播，个人的重播。我们到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕，婚姻如重播。让我们以这一幕中人们陷入个人重播的场景开始。我敢说，这个场景你自己在某个时候也可能经历过。

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:16.360
<v Ira Glass>Carmen and Candido were out just the other night with a friend. And Carmen started telling a story about her nephew, Nikko.
<v Ira Glass>卡门和坎迪多前几天晚上和一位朋友出去。卡门开始讲关于她侄子尼科的故事。

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:21.460
<v Carmen>And as soon as I say, well, when Nikko was three years old, one Sunday. Candido rolls his eyes.
<v Carmen>我刚说，嗯，尼科三岁的时候，有个周日。坎迪多就翻了个白眼。

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:31.440
<v Candido>See, the problem is, that I heard this story like 1,000 times. And I can't sit through it again. Carmen tells the story the exact same way all the time.
<v Candido>你看，问题是，这个故事我听了大概1000遍了。我没法再听下去了。卡门每次讲这个故事的方式完全一样。

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:32.570
<v Carmen>Because it's very funny.
<v Carmen>因为它非常有趣。

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:35.840
<v Candido>She should embellish it for me.
<v Candido>她应该为我润色一下。

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:39.200
<v Ira Glass>Now, Candido, are there stories that you tell over and over?
<v Ira Glass>那么，坎迪多，有没有你会一遍又一遍讲的故事？

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:39.530
<v Candido>No.
<v Candido>没有。

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:41.090
<v Carmen>That's not true.
<v Carmen>那不是真的。

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:42.000
<v Candido>I don't.
<v Candido>我没有。

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:49.160
<v Carmen>That's so not true.
<v Carmen>那太不真实了。

00:32:49.160 --> 00:33:17.660
<v Ira Glass>Yes friends, it is a fact of married life, one that is almost never discussed, that being married, being in any couple for more than one night, I would argue, means hearing the same stories over and over from your partner, possibly for the rest of your life. But have you noticed that of all the stories that could get told in front of each other, only certain stories do get told? Well, to understand the taxonomy of this, the ins and outs of this phenomenon, I spoke with three couples.
<v Ira Glass>是的，朋友们，这是婚姻生活中的一个事实，几乎从不讨论。我认为，结婚，或者说在任何一对伴侣中待上超过一晚，就意味着你要一遍又一遍地听对方讲同样的故事，可能要伴随余生。但你有没有注意到，在所有可以互相讲述的故事中，只有某些故事才会被讲出来？嗯，为了了解这种现象的分类，了解这种现象的来龙去脉，我和三对夫妻聊了聊。

00:33:17.660 --> 00:33:45.490
<v Ira Glass>We begin with Carmen and Candido. There is a story that he tells about the day that he got hurt on the subway and strangers came to his aid. They live in New York City. And this story comes up in any conversation where it might be necessary to prove the point that New Yorkers are not cold hearted but, in fact, helpful and kind to each other. There are the stories that she tells about her nephew Nikko, which come up on when the topic of kids doing adorable things enters a conversation with her friends, usually with friends who have kids. And then there's the graduation story.
<v Ira Glass>我们先从卡门和坎迪多开始。他讲过一个关于他在地铁受伤那天陌生人对他伸出援手的故事。他们住在纽约市。每当需要证明“纽约人并不冷漠，其实互相很有帮助和友善”这个观点时，这个故事就会出现。还有她讲的关于她侄子尼科的故事，每当她的朋友谈论孩子做的可爱事情时，通常是那些有孩子的朋友，这个故事就会出现。然后还有一个毕业典礼的故事。

00:33:45.490 --> 00:33:50.250
<v Candido>Whenever they're talking about embarrassing moments, I guess this is the one that gets told a lot.
<v Candido>每当他们谈论尴尬时刻时，我想这是讲得最多的一个。

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:51.230
<v Carmen>It comes up. Our mother's stories.
<v Carmen>它会出现。我们的母亲的故事。

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:52.310
<v Candido>Our mother's story.
<v Candido>我们母亲的故事。

00:33:52.310 --> 00:33:56.860
<v Carmen>So should I tell my graduation story? Candido rolled his eyes.
<v Carmen>那我要讲我的毕业故事吗？坎迪多翻了个白眼。

00:33:56.860 --> 00:33:59.230
<v Ira Glass>What happens in this story? Yeah, tell me the story.
<v Ira Glass>这个故事里发生了什么？是的，讲给我听听。

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:09.280
<v Carmen>OK, my mother was a teacher. She's now a principal. And she has that personality. Which is great. Very loving but very-- she's an Aries.
<v Carmen>好吧，我妈妈是老师。她现在是校长。她有那种性格。这很好。非常有爱心但非常——她是白羊座。

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:09.900
<v Candido>Aggressive--
<v Candido>咄咄逼人——

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:34.120
<v Carmen>So I went to Catholic school, and when I was graduating from eighth grade, we had our our graduation in the church, in the school's church. And [INAUDIBLE] of those Instamatic Kodak cameras. And she didn't advance the film when my name was called. And I went up there, and I received my diploma, and she didn't get a shot of that. So she goes, go back up there. I was like, mom, you are not making me go back up there.
<v Carmen>所以我上的是教会学校，当我八年级毕业时，我们的毕业典礼在教堂里，在学校的教堂里举行。而且（听不清）那些柯达傻瓜相机。她在我名字被点到时没有推进胶片。我走上去领毕业证书时，她没拍到。所以她说，回去再走一遍。我当时想，妈，你不能让我回去再走一遍。

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:35.020
<v Ira Glass>Oh my God.
<v Ira Glass>天哪。

00:34:35.020 --> 00:34:45.140
<v Carmen>Yeah, completely. In the church, in front of everybody, go back up there right now. I was like, mommy, they're calling other people. And she said, "Father Bradley!" to the priest who was giving the diplomas out.
<v Carmen>是啊，完全是。在教堂里，在所有人面前，现在就回去再走一遍。我当时想，妈咪，他们正在叫别人的名字呢。她说，“布拉德利神父！”冲着正在颁发毕业证书的神父喊。

00:34:45.140 --> 00:34:46.830
<v Ira Glass>Wait, she calls out from the audience?
<v Ira Glass>等等，她从观众席上喊出来？

00:34:46.830 --> 00:35:14.240
<v Carmen>Well, she left her seat, and went up toward the altar. She's by the altar. And she goes, "Father Bradley, just hold on. Go back up there. Go back up there right now." Father Bradley posed, and she took the picture. And she goes, "OK, thank you. Oh, my baby!" And hugging me. Everybody is staring at me. And what I love about that moment, when I think about it, is that my mom didn't care what other people thought.
<v Carmen>嗯，她离开座位，走向祭坛。她就在祭坛边上。她说，“布拉德利神父，等一下。回去再走一遍。现在就回去再走一遍。”布拉德利神父摆了个姿势，她拍了照片。她说，“好的，谢谢。哦，我的宝贝！”然后拥抱着我。每个人都在盯着我。关于那一刻，我想到时所热爱的是，我妈妈根本不在乎别人怎么想。

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:22.880
<v Candido>Exactly.
<v Candido>完全正确。

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:26.690
<v Catherine Hodgman>I've probably heard this story 20 or 25 times.
<v Catherine Hodgman>我大概听过这个故事20或25遍了。

00:35:26.690 --> 00:35:30.880
<v Catherine Hodgman>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN I would guess that she's probably heard it six to eight times.
<v Catherine Hodgman>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 我猜她大概听过六到八遍。

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:33.130
<v Catherine Hodgman>It seems like a lot.
<v Catherine Hodgman>听起来很多。

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:35.440
<v Catherine Hodgman>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN I think she thinks it's pretty funny.
<v Catherine Hodgman>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 我想她觉得这挺有趣的。

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:55.970
<v Ira Glass>This is our second couple, John and Catherine, who've known each other since high school. The story in question happened when they were both 21, both briefly living in England. It's a drinking story to be told while drinking about a night of drinking. John was the one who was drunk in the story. He was young. He was working lousy jobs. He was absolutely bored. So there was a lot of drinking in his life.
<v Ira Glass>这是我们的第二对夫妻，约翰和凯瑟琳，他们从高中起就认识。所说的故事发生在他们21岁的时候，两人都在英国短期居住。这是一个在喝酒时讲的关于某个喝酒夜晚的喝酒故事。故事里喝醉的是约翰。他那时候很年轻。他做着烂工作。他非常无聊。所以他的生活中充满了喝酒。

00:35:55.970 --> 00:36:11.740
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN It was around this time that I also started doing some very low-key shoplifting. And I would take like a pack of gum, or like an onion. And this was very thrilling to me.
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 大概就是在那个时候，我还开始做了一些非常小规模的商店行窃。我会拿一包口香糖，或者一个洋葱。这对我很刺激。

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:30.660
<v Ira Glass>So one night John's friend Charles and another friend suggested they break into the London Zoo. Which at that time turned out to be surprisingly easy to do. They simply hopped over a low iron wrought fence. Once inside, they spotted a pool, with penguins sleeping all around it, right there behind a wall so low that you just step over it.
<v Ira Glass>所以有一天晚上，约翰的朋友查尔斯和另一个朋友建议他们闯入伦敦动物园。当时事实证明那非常容易做到。他们只是跳过了一道低矮的铁栅栏。进入里面后，他们发现了一个池塘，企鹅们在周围睡觉，就在那面低矮的墙后面，你只需跨过去就行。

00:36:30.660 --> 00:37:03.550
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN Yeah, it was exciting, because I'd always liked penguins. I've now learned that they are kind of shifty and mean and also harbingers of doom. I simply stepped over the wall, and I walked down and walked up to a sleeping penguin. Apparently they are diurnal creatures, and they sleep at night. And this one was not very happy about being woken up, because I petted him a little, and he bit me on the finger. It was not a major wound, but it was a token of bad things to come.
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 是的，这很令人兴奋，因为我一直很喜欢企鹅。我现在才知道它们其实有些狡猾而且刻薄，也是厄运的先兆。我只是跨过了那面墙，走过去走到一只睡觉的企鹅面前。显然它们是昼行生物，晚上睡觉。而这只对被吵醒很不高兴，因为我摸了它一下，它咬了我的手指。伤口不算严重，但这是未来不幸的象征。

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:11.560
<v Ira Glass>I have to say, as a symbolic moment, it's a good one. Because something cute has suddenly become deeply un-cute.
<v Ira Glass>我不得不说，作为象征性的时刻，这很棒。因为可爱的东西突然变得极不可爱了。

00:37:11.560 --> 00:37:13.700
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN You find penguins cute?
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 你觉得企鹅可爱？

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:16.370
<v Ira Glass>What are you talking about? Everyone finds penguins cute.
<v Ira Glass>你在说什么？每个人都觉得企鹅可爱。

00:37:16.370 --> 00:37:18.080
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN I look at them differently now.
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 我现在以不同的眼光看它们了。

00:37:18.080 --> 00:37:21.160
<v Ira Glass>It would be like a teddy bear biting you or something.
<v Ira Glass>这就像泰迪熊咬了你一样。

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:33.670
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN Yeah, teddy bears are actually stuffed animals. But as an expert on penguin behavior, you're right, it is very unusual.
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 是的，泰迪熊其实是填充玩具。但作为企鹅行为专家，你是对的，这确实非常不寻常。

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:53.580
<v Ira Glass>What follows turns out to be one of those sprawling stories that takes 45 minutes to tell. So I will choose just a few highlights for you here. There is the run-in with the police. No story like this is complete without the run-in with the police. John is the only one caught. He's questioned. He's taken to jail. He does not tell them his real name. He does not rat out his friends. He tries to act tough with the cops.
<v Ira Glass>接下来发生的事情是一连串要讲45分钟的长篇故事。所以我只为你选几个重点。这里有和警察的冲突。没有像这样的冲突，故事就不完整。约翰是唯一被抓住的。他被审问。他被带到监狱。他没有告诉他们他的真名。他没有出卖他的朋友。他试着在警察面前装硬汉。

00:37:53.580 --> 00:38:24.900
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN They were all laughing at me and saying, what were you doing? Are you Marlin Perkins or something? And they said, "OK, empty your pockets." Now, I had been smart in a way that I haven't been in a long time. Before we went to the zoo, I decided, somewhat presciently, that it would be a good idea for me to not have any identification with me. So I didn't bring my wallet or my keys or my ID.
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 他们都在笑我，说，你在做什么？你是马林·珀金斯吗？他们说，“好吧，清空你的口袋。”现在，我以一种很久没这么做过的方式变得聪明了。去动物园之前，我决定，有点先见之明地认为，不带任何身份证件是个好主意。所以我没带钱包、钥匙或身份证。

00:38:24.900 --> 00:39:00.460
<v Ira Glass>But I did have an onion in my pocket. Which really kind of stopped everyone for a very long moment. It was the only thing that I had. And they said, "What is that for?" And I said, "To feed the animals?" And they seemed to enjoy that. And I kind of thought that this couldn't go on for much longer. But they decided to put me in a cell for a while.
<v Ira Glass>但我口袋里有个洋葱。这确实让所有人在那一刻愣了很久。这是我身上唯一的东西。他们说，“那是干什么用的？”我说，“喂动物？”他们似乎很喜欢这个。我当时想这不可能再持续多久了。但他们决定把我关在牢房里一会儿。

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:13.610
<v Ira Glass>An on and on, you get the idea. What's strikingly fascinating about hearing his wife, Catherine, tell this same story is that after hearing it at least 20 times-- Well, you'll hear.
<v Ira Glass>就这样，你明白了吧。最引人入胜的一点是，当他的妻子凯瑟琳讲同一个故事时，在那至少听了20遍之后——嗯，你会听到的。

00:39:13.610 --> 00:39:32.060
<v Catherine Hodgman>They had a good time for a while, and then, at some point, they were in the pig pen. And I don't know, they knew somehow that the police were there. Isn't funny that I am not really sure how this transpired?
<v Catherine Hodgman>他们玩了一会儿，然后，在某个时候，他们在猪圈里。我不确定，他们以某种方式知道警察在附近。不可笑吗，我其实不确定这是怎么发生的？

00:39:32.060 --> 00:39:33.740
<v Ira Glass>Do what animal bit John?
<v Ira Glass>是什么动物咬了约翰？

00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:35.500
<v Catherine Hodgman>A pig.
<v Catherine Hodgman>猪。

00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:38.180
<v Ira Glass>OK, that's not right.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，那不对。

00:39:38.180 --> 00:39:41.770
<v Catherine Hodgman>You're kidding?
<v Catherine Hodgman>你在开玩笑吗？

00:39:41.770 --> 00:39:43.280
<v Ira Glass>Nope, it's not a pig.
<v Ira Glass>不，那不是猪。

00:39:43.280 --> 00:39:53.400
<v Catherine Hodgman>I thought he was in the pig pen. And there's this whole thing where he's like, the pig is licking my ear.
<v Catherine Hodgman>我以为他在猪圈里。还有整个事情，他说，猪在舔我的耳朵。

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:55.190
<v Ira Glass>Nope. Not the pig.
<v Ira Glass>不。不是猪。

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:58.250
<v Catherine Hodgman>Was the pig licking Charles's ear?
<v Catherine Hodgman>是猪在舔查尔斯的耳朵吗？

00:39:58.250 --> 00:40:01.960
<v Ira Glass>You barely have this story. You barely know this story.
<v Ira Glass>你几乎没记住这个故事。你几乎不知道这个故事。

00:40:01.960 --> 00:40:07.690
<v Catherine Hodgman>I know. I do barely know this story. Do you think that I barely listen? Maybe I don't listen any more?
<v Catherine Hodgman>我知道。我确实几乎不知道这个故事。你觉得我几乎不听吗？也许我不再听了？

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:23.660
<v Ira Glass>The part that she actually does remember every time she tells it is the part where he's in jail. She says it brings out some sort of maternal feeling in her. She just can't stand the thought of him going through that. What they're both clear on is why this is the story that John has told over and over for more than a decade. Here is John explaining.
<v Ira Glass>每次她讲的时候，她真正记住的那部分是他被关在监狱里的那段。她说这激发了她内心的某种母性。她简直受不了他经历那一切的想法。他们都很清楚为什么这是约翰十多年来一遍又一遍讲的故事。这是约翰的解释。

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:41.470
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN I think that the story, it's a version of myself that seems a little reckless, adventurous, quick-witted, but also ultimately humiliated by my own overreach.
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 我认为这个故事，它是我的一个版本，看起来有点鲁莽、冒险、机智，但也最终因为自己的自负而蒙受羞辱。

00:40:41.470 --> 00:40:50.630
<v Ira Glass>Have you noticed that there's certain people that all the stories they tell, they are triumphant, and then other people, all the stories that they tell about themselves, they are humiliated.
<v Ira Glass>你有没有注意到，有些人讲的所有故事都是他们胜利的，而另一些人讲的所有关于他们自己的故事，他们都是被羞辱的。

00:40:50.630 --> 00:41:07.820
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) JOHN HODGMAN See, I think a good drinking story contains both. Both that heroic aspect and that completely de-clothed, humiliated, embarrassed aspect as well.
<v Ira Glass>（采访对象）约翰·霍奇曼 瞧，我认为一个好的喝酒故事应该兼具两者。既有英雄的一面，也有完全赤裸、羞辱、尴尬的一面。

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:22.490
<v Ira Glass>When I was talking to him about it, I asked him, why is this the story that gets told over and over? And he said, that a really good story like this should be something where the person telling the story both appears as a hero and then as an idiotic fool.
<v Ira Glass>当我和他谈起这一点时，我问他，为什么这是那个一遍又一遍被讲的故事？他说，一个像这样的好故事应该让讲故事的人既看起来像英雄，又看起来像个白痴。

00:41:22.490 --> 00:41:38.340
<v Catherine Hodgman>[LAUGHTER] Well, where's the hero part?
<v Catherine Hodgman>（笑）好吧，英雄的部分在哪儿？

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:57.030
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to our last couple, Robert and Tamar. They also have a story that gets repeated in their house that has been the source of a small difference in interpretation between them. It's a story they both tell. Though him more than her, you get feeling. They don't tell the story the same way. And we will start with her version of the story.
<v Ira Glass>这就引出了我们的最后一对，罗伯特和塔玛。他们家里也有一个会被重复讲的故事，这成了他们之间解释存在小分歧的来源。这是他们俩都讲的故事。虽然他讲的比她多，你会有这种感觉。他们讲故事的方式不完全一样。我们将从她的版本开始。

00:41:57.030 --> 00:42:36.000
<v Tamar Lewin>The first thing you need to know about this story is that I am totally celebrity blind, just completely. So much of my life with Robert has been wandering around New York and him saying, oh, look you were sitting next to Candice Bergen. And I'd say, no I wasn't. And he's always right, and I'm always wrong. So I'm really pleased one day. I'm out all by myself in the world, and I'm on the East Side, and I'm walking down Madison Avenue. And I see someone, and I know-- me the celebrity blind person-- I know absolutely for sure, for sure that this person across the street is Jackie Kennedy. And not only is it Jackie Kennedy, but she's looking at me. And she has her hand up when I smile at her.
<v Tamar Lewin>关于这个故事你需要知道的第一件事是我完全是脸盲，彻彻底底的。我和罗伯特的生活大部分时间都在纽约漫步，他会说，哦，看，你刚才坐在坎迪斯·伯根旁边。我会说，不，我没坐。而且他总是对的，我总是错的。所以有一天我真的很高兴。我独自一人在世界上，我在东区，我走在麦迪逊大道上。我看到一个人，我认识——我这个脸盲的人——我绝对确信，绝对确信街对面那个人是杰奎琳·肯尼迪。不仅是杰奎琳·肯尼迪，而且她在看我。当我向她微笑时，她举起了手。

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:44.200
<v Ira Glass>OK, let's stop that right there. Before she gets too far, here is Robert's interpretation of the same event. Their interviews were recorded separately.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，到此为止。在她走得太远之前，这是罗伯特对同一事件的解释。他们的采访是分开录制的。

00:42:44.200 --> 00:43:13.150
<v Robert Krulwich>It's a beautiful, beautiful fall day. And we're walking down Fifth Avenue. The Central Park is on our right. I just picture this very, very precisely. And we're walking along, and Tamar is distracted. She looks over her left shoulder, and she goes [SOUND OF SURPISE]. There I see, across the street, Jackie Onassis, President Kennedy's wife, and she's waving, very modestly, at Tamar.
<v Robert Krulwich>这是一个美丽、美丽的秋天。我们走在第五大道上。中央公园在我们右边。我非常、非常准确地记得这个场景。我们走着走着，塔玛走神了。她转头看向左肩，然后发出（惊讶的声音）。在那里，我看到街对面是杰奎琳·奥纳西斯，肯尼迪总统的妻子，她正在非常谦虚地向塔玛挥手。

00:43:13.150 --> 00:43:26.040
<v Ira Glass>You've probably noticed the key differences already. He says that they're together. She says that she's alone. He says, next to Central Park. She says, Madison Avenue. But once she spots Jackie O, the stories fly in tandem for a while.
<v Ira Glass>你可能已经注意到关键的区别了。他说他们在一起。她说她独自一人。他说靠近中央公园。她说麦迪逊大道。但一旦她发现了杰奎琳·O，故事就同步了一会儿。

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:31.080
<v Tamar Lewin>She has her hand when I smile at her, and she waves at me.
<v Tamar Lewin>当我向她微笑时，她举起了手，然后向我挥手。

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:37.800
<v Robert Krulwich>And I thought, oh my God. I didn't know that they knew each other, whatever. And I'm looking at Tamar, and Tamar's looking at Jackie Onassis.
<v Robert Krulwich>我想，哦，我的上帝。我不知道她们认识，不管怎样。我在看塔玛，塔玛在看杰奎琳·奥纳西斯。

00:43:37.800 --> 00:43:46.890
<v Tamar Lewin>And I'm so excited. And I wave back sort of tentatively, but beaming, beaming, beaming. And she waves back more so. I then wave back with my whole, whole heart.
<v Tamar Lewin>我太兴奋了。我有点试探性地回挥，但光芒四射，光芒四射。她挥得更厉害了。然后我全心全意地回挥。

00:43:46.890 --> 00:43:57.040
<v Robert Krulwich>So I'm just staring at this in wonder. And then Jackie raises her hand even more excitedly and starts sort of moving it back and forth and back and forth.
<v Robert Krulwich>所以我正惊奇地盯着这景象。然后杰奎琳更兴奋地举起手，开始来回摆动，来回摆动。

00:43:57.040 --> 00:44:00.680
<v Tamar Lewin>And I'm waving, and beaming, and I'm so happy and proud.
<v Tamar Lewin>我在挥手，在发光，我感到如此快乐和骄傲。

00:44:00.680 --> 00:44:15.760
<v Robert Krulwich>And in that moment, a cab pulls up alongside Jackie Onassis. And what Jackie Onassis had actually been doing is just waving for a cab. And my wife, by mistake, somehow thought that Jackie was waving at her and is feeling really stupid.
<v Robert Krulwich>就在那一刻，一辆出租车开到杰奎琳·奥纳西斯身边。而杰奎琳·奥纳西斯实际上所做的是仅仅是在打车。我妻子，出于误解，不知怎么以为杰奎琳是在向她挥手，感觉自己真的很蠢。

00:44:15.760 --> 00:44:18.630
<v Tamar Lewin>And so I'm really, pretty humiliated.
<v Tamar Lewin>所以我真的，相当羞辱。

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:19.820
<v Robert Krulwich>As am I.
<v Robert Krulwich>我也是。

00:44:19.820 --> 00:44:28.750
<v Tamar Lewin>Because many people had been looking at Jackie Kennedy and many people had been looking at me making a fool of myself, waving, waving, waving, waving.
<v Tamar Lewin>因为很多人一直在看着杰奎琳·肯尼迪，很多人一直在看着我出丑，挥手，挥手，挥手，挥手。

00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:32.180
<v Robert Krulwich>And so we laugh about it, and we head downtown.
<v Robert Krulwich>所以我们对此笑了笑，然后前往市中心。

00:44:32.180 --> 00:44:57.410
<v Ira Glass>So we laugh about it, and we head downtown. Now that is where his version of the story ends, a moment of love, a moment of togetherness. Tamar's version of the story continues. In her version, she comes home. Remember, she experienced the whole thing alone in her version. She comes home, and she tells Robert what happened to her. Weeks later, in her version, they are at somebody's house for dinner, and Robert just launches into his version of the story, the version that you just heard.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们对此笑了笑，然后前往市中心。好了，他的故事版本到这里结束了，这是爱的时刻，在一起的时刻。塔玛的故事版本还在继续。在她的版本中，她回到了家。记住，在这个版本中她独自经历了这一切。她回到家，告诉罗伯特在她身上发生了什么。几周后，在她的版本中，他们在某人家吃晚饭，罗伯特径直讲起了他的版本，就是你们刚才听到的那个版本。

00:44:57.410 --> 00:45:08.500
<v Tamar Lewin>As we leave that house, I say, you know Robert, you weren't there. And he said, "No. But I remember it, I can picture it, I can see it so clearly." And I say, "But you weren't there."
<v Tamar Lewin>当我们离开那栋房子时，我说，你知道罗伯特，你当时不在场。他说，“不。但我记得它，我能想象它，我能看得那么清楚。”我说，“但你当时不在场。”

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:55.610
<v Robert Krulwich>She says that I wasn't there. Which is astonishing to me. I mean, this is like I can feel this on my skin. I have told this story with such vividness because I remember it so vividly. I just remember things. Like things, like the way the sun was catching leaves. I remember turning around. I remember the intake of breath and the surprise. I remember all the little things going on in my mind . How do they know each other? Oh my God. She said, I wasn't there. I was never there. But she told it to me, and I, just simply, sort of like Ghengis Khan or Alexander the Great I occupied it. Like it was real estate that I wanted to be part of, so I just marched in and became part of it.
<v Robert Krulwich>她说我当时不在场。这让我很震惊。我是说，这就像我能感觉到它在我皮肤上一样。我之所以把这个故事讲得如此生动，是因为我记得它如此生动。我只是记得事情。比如事情，比如太阳照在叶子上的方式。我记得转过身。我记得吸气声和惊喜。我记得我脑子里发生的所有小事。她们怎么认识？哦，我的上帝。她说，我当时不在场。我从没在场。但她告诉了我，我就，简直就像成吉思汗或亚历山大大帝一样，占领了它。就像我想成为一部分的房产，所以我直接走进去，成了它的一部分。

00:45:55.610 --> 00:45:59.090
<v Ira Glass>Do you believe her, that you weren't in it?
<v Ira Glass>你相信她吗，你当时没在场？

00:45:59.090 --> 00:46:30.850
<v Robert Krulwich>Yeah. Yeah. Because I live, as all married people do, in a courthouse. And the jury, upon deliberating about this, said, this particular witness has proven over the years a complete-- and she is very, very believable. A credible witness has testified. And you sir? Over the years, we have formed our own opinions about you. Judgment to the wife.
<v Robert Krulwich>是的。是的。因为我生活在一个法庭里，像所有已婚人士一样。陪审团在商议后说，这位特定的证人多年来证明了一个完全——而且她非常、非常可信。一位可信的证人作证了。那你呢，先生？多年来，我们对你也有了自己的看法。判决归妻子。

00:46:30.850 --> 00:46:33.550
<v Tamar Lewin>So now it's become a shared story.
<v Tamar Lewin>所以现在它变成了一个共享的故事。

00:46:33.550 --> 00:46:37.610
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, and he'll tell it, and in his version, he's in the story?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，他会讲这个故事，而在他的版本里，他在故事里？

00:46:37.610 --> 00:46:44.100
<v Tamar Lewin>In this version he has his view of what happened, but he says, but actually Tamar says I wasn't there.
<v Tamar Lewin>在这个版本里，他对发生的事情有自己的看法，但他会说，但其实塔玛说我当时不在场。

00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:52.970
<v Ira Glass>Well thank you to all of our couple's, Carmen Rivera and Candido Tirado, John and Catherine Hodgman, and finally Tamar Lewin and Robert Krulwich.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，谢谢我们所有的夫妻，卡门·里维拉和坎迪多·蒂拉多，约翰和凯瑟琳·霍奇曼，最后是塔玛·卢温和罗伯特·克鲁维奇。

00:46:52.970 --> 00:46:53.490
<v Ira Glass>Robert?
<v Ira Glass>罗伯特？

00:46:53.490 --> 00:46:54.160
<v Robert Krulwich>Yeah.
<v Robert Krulwich>是啊。

00:46:54.160 --> 00:46:57.590
<v Ira Glass>If the story about Jackie Kennedy is true, you be able to tell me what she was wearing.
<v Ira Glass>如果关于杰奎琳·肯尼迪的故事是真的，你能告诉我她穿着什么吗？

00:46:57.590 --> 00:47:16.970
<v Robert Krulwich>Yeah, oh yeah. She was wearing a suit. She's wearing a suit, it had white buttons, and it had a collar, and it was a skirt, and I couldn't see her feet. But I remember that she had white buttons. You know where the buttons are attached to the garment, the stitching was black.
<v Robert Krulwich>是啊，哦，是的。她穿着西装。她穿着西装，有白色纽扣，还有衣领，是裙装，我看不到她的脚。但我记得她有白色纽扣。你知道纽扣缝在衣服上的地方，缝线是黑色的。

00:47:16.970 --> 00:47:19.000
<v Ira Glass>OK, you were too far away to see that.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，你离得太远了，看不到那个。

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:35.840
<v Robert Krulwich>Yeah, exactly. I never stopped to wonder about the zoom lens that I apparently had handy at that time.
<v Robert Krulwich>是啊，没错。我从没想过我当时随手拿到的那个变焦镜头。

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:58.630
<v Robert Krulwich>[MUSIC - "FIVE TIMES OUT OF A HUNDRED" BY HOT HOT HEAT]
<v Robert Krulwich>（音乐 - 热门热门热的《百分之五》）

00:47:58.630 --> 00:48:19.320
<v Ira Glass>Act 3, Reruns At The Back Of The Bus. But we are a nation that keeps re-running for ourselves a certain story about ourselves and about Rosa Parks. Not only ago there was even a small bit of controversy when the movie Barbershop chose to depart from the official fable that we usually tell ourselves. Well, Sarah Vowell has been watching the trend carefully wherever it appears.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，车尾的重播。但我们是一个不断为自己重播关于我们自己和罗莎·帕克斯的故事的国家。不久前，当电影《理发店》选择背离我们通常告诉自己的官方寓言时，甚至还引起了一点争议。嗯，莎拉·沃威尔一直在仔细观察这种趋势出现的地方。

00:48:19.320 --> 00:49:00.360
<v Sarah Vowell>According to Reuters, on January 20, 2001 in Washington DC, the special guest at the Florida State inaugural ball was introduced by the country singer Larry Gatlin. He said, in France it was Joan of Arc, in the Crimea it was Florence Nightingale, in the deep South there was Rosa Parks, in India there was Mother Teresa, and in Florida there was Katherine Harris. I leave it to my Indian, Crimean, and French colleagues to determine how the Florida Secretary of State is or is not similar to Teresa, Florence, or Saint Joan.
<v Sarah Vowell>据路透社报道，2001年1月20日在华盛顿特区，佛罗里达州就职舞会的特别嘉宾由乡村歌手拉里·盖特林介绍。他说，在法国有圣女贞德，在克里米亚有弗罗伦斯·南丁格尔，在南方深处有罗莎·帕克斯，在印度有特蕾莎修女，在佛罗里达州有凯瑟琳·哈里斯。我留给我的印度、克里米亚和法国同事去决定佛罗里达州州务卿在多大程度上与特蕾莎、弗罗伦斯或圣女贞德相似。

00:49:00.360 --> 00:49:36.390
<v Sarah Vowell>As for Rosa Parks, Katherine Harris can get in line. Because people around here can't stop comparing themselves to Parks. To wit, the Mayor of Friendship Heights Maryland has proposed an outdoor smoking ban because, according to the Washington Post , citizens with asthma or other illnesses cannot have full access to areas where smokers are doing their evil deed. The mayor compares this horrific possibility to Rosa Parks being sent to the back of the bus.
<v Sarah Vowell>至于罗莎·帕克斯，凯瑟琳·哈里斯可以排队了。因为这里的人无法停止把自己和帕克斯相提并论。据悉，马里兰州友谊高地的市长提议禁止户外吸烟，因为据《华盛顿邮报》报道，患有哮喘或其他疾病的公民无法完全进入吸烟者进行邪恶行为的区域。市长将这种可怕的可能性比作罗莎·帕克斯被送往公交车后排。

00:49:36.390 --> 00:50:30.490
<v Sarah Vowell>A California dairy farmer, protesting the government's milk pricing system, poured milk down a drain in front of TV cameras claiming that he had to take a stand just like Rosa Parks had to take a stand. A street performer in Saint Augustine, Florida is challenging a city ordinance that bans him from doing his act on the town's historic Saint George Street. The performers lawyer told the Florida Times-Union, telling these people they can exercise their first amendment rights somewhere other than on Saint George is like telling Rosa Parks that she has to sit in the back of the bus. Which is, coincidentally, also the argument of another Florida lawyer. This one representing adult dancers contesting Tampa's ordinance outlawing lap dancing.
<v Sarah Vowell>一位加州奶农，为了抗议政府的牛奶定价系统，在电视摄像机前将牛奶倒入下水道，声称他必须像罗莎·帕克斯一样表明立场。佛罗里达州圣奥古斯丁的一位街头艺人正在挑战一项禁止他在该镇历史悠久的圣乔治街表演的城市法令。这位表演者的律师告诉《佛罗里达时代联盟》，告诉这些人他们可以在圣乔治街以外的地方行使第一修正案权利，就像告诉罗莎·帕克斯她必须坐在公交车后排一样。这巧合地也是另一位佛罗里达州律师的论点。这位律师代表挑战坦帕市取缔膝上舞法令的成人舞者。

00:50:30.490 --> 00:51:00.870
<v Sarah Vowell>I would also like to mention the rocker, marksman, and conservative activist Ted Nugent who, in his autobiography, God , Guns, and Rock and Roll , refers to himself as Rosa Parks with a loud guitar. Which is so inaccurate. Everyone knows he's more like Mary Matalin with a fancy deer rifle.
<v Sarah Vowell>我还想提一下摇滚歌手、射击手和保守派活动家泰德·纽金特，他在自传《上帝、枪支和摇滚》中称自己为“带着响亮吉他的罗莎·帕克斯”。这太不准确了。大家都知道他更像带着一把花哨猎枪的玛丽·马塔林。

00:51:00.870 --> 00:51:20.760
<v Sarah Vowell>Call me picky, but breathing secondhand smoke, being subject to unfair dairy pricing, and not being able to mime or lap dance, though they are all tragic, tragic injustices, are not quite as bad as the systematic segregation of public transportation based on skin color.
<v Sarah Vowell>别说我挑剔，呼吸二手烟，遭受不公平的牛奶定价，以及不能模仿或跳膝上舞，尽管这些都是悲剧，是悲剧性的不公正，但它们并不像基于肤色的公共交通系统性隔离那样糟糕。

00:51:20.760 --> 00:52:18.670
<v Sarah Vowell>And while fighting for your right to lap dance and mime, and breathe just the regular pollution and not the cigarette smokers, is a very fine, very American idea, it is not quite as brave as being a middle-aged black woman in Alabama in 1955 telling a white man she's not giving him her seat despite the fact that the law requires her to do so. And oh, by the way, in the process, she gets arrested. And then sparks the Montgomery Bus Boycott, which is the seed of the Civil Rights Movement as we know it. The Bus Boycotters not only introduced a 26-year-old pastor by the name of Martin Luther King junior into national public life, but after many months of car pools, walking, and court fights against bus segregation, got the separate-but-equal doctrine declared illegal once and for all.
<v Sarah Vowell>虽然为了你有权跳膝上舞和模仿，以及呼吸正常的空气而不是香烟烟雾而奋斗是一个非常棒、非常美国式的想法，但它并不像1955年阿拉巴马州一位中年黑人妇女告诉白人她不会给他让座，尽管法律要求她这样做那样勇敢。哦，顺便说一下，在此过程中，她被捕了。然后引发了蒙哥马利公交车抵制运动，这是我们所知的民权运动的种子。公交车抵制者不仅将一位名叫马丁·路德·金二世的26岁牧师引入了国家公共生活，而且经过数月的拼车、步行和反对公交车隔离的法律斗争，最终使“隔离但平等”的原则被永久宣布为非法。

00:52:18.670 --> 00:52:52.180
<v Sarah Vowell>It's not just the people on the right, like Katherine Harris and Ted Nugent, who seem especially silly being likened to Parks. I first cringed at this analogy trend at the lefty Ralph Nader's October 2000 campaign rally in Madison Square Garden. Ever sit in a coliseum full of people who think they're heroes? I was surrounded by thousands of well meaning well fed white kids who loved it when the filmmaker Michael Moore told them they should, like Rosa Parks, stand up to power.
<v Sarah Vowell>不仅仅是右派人士，如凯瑟琳·哈里斯和泰德·纽金特，把自己比作帕克斯显得特别愚蠢。我第一次对这种类比趋势感到畏缩是在拉尔夫·纳德2000年10月在麦迪逊广场花园的竞选集会上。有没有坐进过充满以为自己是英雄的人的竞技场？我被成千上万心地善良、吃得饱饱的白人孩子包围着，当电影制作人迈克尔·摩尔告诉他们应该像罗莎·帕克斯一样勇敢面对强权时，他们喜欢极了。

00:52:52.180 --> 00:52:59.300
<v Michael Moore>: What if Rosa Parks had said to herself, I'm the only person on this bus, I can't win? I'm afraid.
<v Michael Moore>：如果罗莎·帕克斯当时对自己说，我是这辆车上唯一的人，我赢不了？我很害怕，那会怎么样？

00:52:59.300 --> 00:53:38.960
<v Sarah Vowell>By which I think he meant, vote for Nader so he could qualify for federal matching funds. I think I'm a fine enough person. By the very next morning I was having people over for waffles. But I hope I'm not being falsely modest by pointing out that I'm no Harriet Tubman and I'm certainly no Rosa Parks. As far as I'm concerned, about the only person in recent memory who has an unimpeachable right to compare himself to Parks is that Chinese student who stared down those tanks in Tiananmen Square.
<v Sarah Vowell>我想他的意思是，投票给纳德，这样他就能获得联邦匹配基金。我觉得我是一个足够好的人。第二天早上我还请人吃华夫饼。但我希望我没有通过指出我不是哈里特·塔布曼，当然也不是罗莎·帕克斯而表现得过于谦虚。就我而言，记忆中最近唯一一个有无可争辩的权利把自己比作帕克斯的人，是那个站在天安门广场面对坦克群的中国学生。

00:53:38.960 --> 00:54:09.350
<v Sarah Vowell>I was reminded of those Naderites watching a rerun of the sitcom Sports Night on Comedy Central . Dan, a television sportscaster played by Josh Charles, has been ordered by his network to make an on-air apology to viewers because he said in a magazine interview that he supports the legalization of marijuana. He stands by his opinion and balks at apologizing. His boss, Isaac, played by Robert Guillaume, agrees but tells him to do it anyway.
<v Sarah Vowell>看着喜剧中心频道的喜剧片《体育之夜》的重播，我想起了那些纳德的支持者。由乔什·查尔斯饰演的丹是一名电视体育评论员，被网络要求向观众进行电视道歉，因为他在杂志采访中表示支持大麻合法化。他坚持自己的观点，拒绝道歉。由罗伯特·吉劳姆饰演的老板艾萨克同意这一点，但让他无论如何都要这么做。

00:54:09.350 --> 00:54:12.640
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>Because this is television and this is how it's done.
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>因为这是电视，这就是它的运作方式。

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:18.780
<v Josh Charles In Sports Night>Yeah, well, sitting in the back of the bus was how it was done until a 42-year-old lady moved up front. I'm not very impressed with how things are done, Isaac.
<v Josh Charles In Sports Night>是的，好吧，坐在公交车后排也是它的运作方式，直到一位42岁的女士搬到了前排。我对事情的运作方式印象不深，艾萨克。

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:22.920
<v Sarah Vowell>A few minutes later Isaac looks Dan in the eye and tells him--
<v Sarah Vowell>几分钟后，艾萨克注视着丹的眼睛告诉他——

00:54:22.920 --> 00:54:27.460
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>Danny, you know I love you, don't you?
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>丹尼，你知道我爱你，对吧？

00:54:27.460 --> 00:54:28.310
<v Josh Charles In Sports Night>Yeah.
<v Josh Charles In Sports Night>是啊。

00:54:28.310 --> 00:54:40.960
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>And because I love you, I can say this. No rich young white guy has ever gotten anywhere with me comparing himself to Rosa Parks. Got it?
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>因为我爱你，所以我可以说这个。没有哪个富有的年轻白人因为把自己比作罗莎·帕克斯而对我有什么意义。懂吗？

00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:42.190
<v Josh Charles In Sports Night>Yes, sir.
<v Josh Charles In Sports Night>是的，长官。

00:54:42.190 --> 00:54:44.310
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>Good.
<v Robert Guillaume In Sports Night>很好。

00:54:44.310 --> 00:55:36.630
<v Sarah Vowell>Finally, the voice of reason. Which, of course, was heard on a canceled network TV series airing on cable. Analogies give order to the world and solidarity. Pointing out how one person is like another is reassuring, less lonely. Maybe those who would compare their personal inconveniences to the epic struggles of history are just looking for company. And who wouldn't want to be in the company of Rosa Parks. On the other hand, perhaps people who compare themselves to Rosa Parks are simply arrogant, pampered nincompoops with delusions of grandeur who couldn't tell the difference between a paper cut and decapitation.
<v Sarah Vowell>终于，理性之声出现了。当然，这是在有线电视上播出的被取消的网络电视系列节目中听到的。类比赋予世界秩序和团结。指出一个人如何像另一个人是令人安心的，不那么孤独。也许那些将个人不便与历史的史诗斗争相提并论的人只是在寻找同伴。谁不想和罗莎·帕克斯为伍呢？另一方面，也许那些把自己比作罗莎·帕克斯的人只不过是傲慢、宠坏、有着宏大妄想，连纸割伤和斩首区别都分不清的傻瓜。

00:55:36.630 --> 00:56:10.270
<v Sarah Vowell>In defense of Ted Nugent, the street performer, the mayor, the dairy farmer, the lap dancers, the Naderites, and a fictional sportscaster, I will point out that Katherine Harris is the only person on my list of people lamely compared to a civil rights icon who, at the very moment she was being compared to a civil rights icon, was actually being sued for massive voter disenfranchisement of people of color during the presidential election by the NAACP.
<v Sarah Vowell>为了给泰德·纽金特、街头艺人、市长、奶农、膝上舞者、纳德的支持者和一位虚构的体育评论员辩护，我会指出凯瑟琳·哈里斯是我名单上所有被拙劣地比作民权偶像的人中，唯一一个在被比作民权偶像的时刻，正因为在总统选举期间大规模剥夺有色人种选民权利而被全国有色人种协进会起诉的人。

